# Dart Sentinel — Architecture Enforcement

This project uses Dart Sentinel for deterministic architecture enforcement.
All rules are defined in `analyzer.yaml` at the project root.

## Workflow

1. **Before writing code**: Call `get_architecture` from dart-sentinel MCP to understand layers and boundaries.
2. **Before adding imports**: Call `check_import` to verify the import is allowed.
3. **After writing code**: Call `analyze_file` on every new or modified file. Fix ALL violations.

## Architecture Rules

- **Layer boundaries** are enforced — check `analyzer.yaml` for allowed dependencies between layers.
- **Feature isolation** is enforced — features cannot import from other features. Use shared/core layers.
- **Banned symbols** are enforced — use design system components instead of raw Flutter widgets.
- **Model classes** in model paths must include `toMap`, `fromMap`, `copyWith`, `==`, `hashCode`, `toString`.

## Code Quality Rules

- Dispose all resources (StreamSubscription, TextEditingController, AnimationController)
- No empty catch blocks — handle, rethrow, or document why it's safe to ignore
- No `setState`/`context` usage after `await` without `mounted` check
- Keep cyclomatic complexity under 10 per method
- Keep methods under 50 lines, files under 300 lines
- No generic names (`data`, `result`, `value`, `temp`) — use descriptive names

## MCP Tools

| Tool | When to use |
|------|-------------|
| `get_architecture` | Before any architectural decision |
| `check_import` | Before adding any import |
| `analyze_file` | After creating or modifying any file |
| `analyze` | For full project scan |
| `impact_analysis` | Before refactoring to understand blast radius |

Treat Sentinel violations as blocking — equivalent to compile errors.
